1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,960
Somewhere in your home, there
might be a clock that nobody has ever set.

2
00:00:04,576 --> 00:00:06,336
It survives power cuts.

3
00:00:07,056 --> 00:00:11,176
It changes for daylight saving time
on its own, in the middle of the night.

4
00:00:11,778 --> 00:00:13,738
It has no internet connection.

5
00:00:13,998 --> 00:00:17,578
No Wi-Fi, no GPS, no app on your phone.

6
00:00:18,212 --> 00:00:20,732
All it has is a tiny radio receiver.

7
00:00:20,812 --> 00:00:24,192
And once every second,
it hears a single pulse.

8
00:00:24,945 --> 00:00:27,105
So how does a clock
turn one pulse per second

9
00:00:27,335 --> 00:00:30,805
into the exact time, the date,
and even the day of the week?

10
00:00:32,535 --> 00:00:37,055
In Europe, those pulses come
from DCF77, near Frankfurt.

11
00:00:37,875 --> 00:00:41,075
In America, from WWVB, in Colorado.

12
00:00:42,135 --> 00:00:44,395
Different transmitters, the same idea.

13
00:00:45,029 --> 00:00:46,509
Take the German one.

14
00:00:47,109 --> 00:00:49,989
It sends a steady radio wave,
day and night.

15
00:00:50,729 --> 00:00:54,189
And at the start of every second,
it briefly turns that wave down.

16
00:00:54,815 --> 00:00:57,135
The trick is how long it stays down.

17
00:00:57,491 --> 00:01:00,211
A dip of a tenth of a second means zero.

18
00:01:00,891 --> 00:01:02,871
A dip of two tenths means one.

19
00:01:03,477 --> 00:01:07,197
That's the whole idea.
No packets, no network.

20
00:01:07,557 --> 00:01:11,157
Just a radio wave that dips
for a short time, or a longer one.

21
00:01:13,387 --> 00:01:14,947
One bit every second.

22
00:01:15,027 --> 00:01:18,907
Which means it would take a whole minute
to send just sixty bits.

23
00:01:19,371 --> 00:01:21,331
And in fact, it sends only fifty-nine.

24
00:01:22,325 --> 00:01:26,085
In the last second of every minute,
the wave doesn't dip at all.

25
00:01:26,745 --> 00:01:30,745
And that missing dip is the most
important part of the entire signal.

26
00:01:31,125 --> 00:01:35,505
Because when the next dip arrives, the
clock knows a new minute has just begun.

27
00:01:36,235 --> 00:01:38,235
The gap works like a full stop.

28
00:01:38,795 --> 00:01:41,915
Everything between two gaps
is one complete sentence.

29
00:01:44,645 --> 00:01:47,465
So what fits into a sentence
of fifty-nine bits?

30
00:01:48,175 --> 00:01:49,915
The minute. The hour.

31
00:01:50,215 --> 00:01:53,295
The day of the month, the day of the week,
the month, and the year.

32
00:01:53,927 --> 00:01:55,687
Most numbers are sent digit by digit.

33
00:01:55,917 --> 00:01:59,757
Forty-seven minutes
isn't sent as forty-seven.

34
00:01:59,987 --> 00:02:03,507
It's sent as two separate digits,
a four and a seven.

35
00:02:04,073 --> 00:02:07,233
Four bits are enough for any digit
from zero to nine.

36
00:02:07,633 --> 00:02:11,693
The tens of minutes never go past five,
so they get only three.

37
00:02:11,999 --> 00:02:13,919
And the year gets just two digits.

38
00:02:14,739 --> 00:02:19,429
As far as the transmitter is concerned,
2026 is simply twenty-six.

39
00:02:19,779 --> 00:02:22,239
The clock supplies the century itself.

40
00:02:24,659 --> 00:02:26,819
But radio isn't a clean channel.

41
00:02:27,419 --> 00:02:30,639
A motor, a phone charger,
a cheap LED bulb...

42
00:02:30,819 --> 00:02:32,639
any of them can smudge a dip.

43
00:02:32,884 --> 00:02:37,504
So after each block of numbers
comes one extra bit, called a parity bit.

44
00:02:37,964 --> 00:02:42,244
It's chosen so that the number of ones
in the block always comes out even.

45
00:02:42,496 --> 00:02:44,776
If interference flips a single bit,

46
00:02:44,856 --> 00:02:48,396
the count turns odd, and the clock knows
something went wrong.

47
00:02:48,649 --> 00:02:52,769
But if two bits flip, the count is even
again, and the mistake slips through.

48
00:02:53,375 --> 00:02:56,235
That's why many clocks
don't trust a single minute.

49
00:02:56,875 --> 00:02:58,815
They wait for another valid one.

50
00:02:59,039 --> 00:03:01,939
And that's why setting itself
can take several minutes,

51
00:03:02,019 --> 00:03:04,059
and on a bad day, much longer.

52
00:03:05,261 --> 00:03:07,241
Which leaves a strange problem.

53
00:03:08,381 --> 00:03:12,201
The clock spends a whole minute
collecting the time for ten forty-eight.

54
00:03:12,421 --> 00:03:15,981
But while it's collecting that message,
it's still ten forty-seven.

55
00:03:17,289 --> 00:03:19,249
So when does it actually change?

56
00:03:19,752 --> 00:03:21,772
Because the message is an announcement.

57
00:03:22,612 --> 00:03:25,472
It says what the time will be
when the next minute begins.

58
00:03:26,271 --> 00:03:29,771
The clock reads the whole sentence,
waits out the missing dip,

59
00:03:29,871 --> 00:03:33,271
and when the next dip arrives,
that dip is the exact moment.

60
00:03:33,962 --> 00:03:36,282
The bits tell the clock what time it is.

61
00:03:36,962 --> 00:03:38,342
The dip tells it when.

62
00:03:40,992 --> 00:03:44,312
But the sentence has a few more words
than just the time.

63
00:03:44,907 --> 00:03:48,827
Two bits say whether Germany
is on winter time or summer time.

64
00:03:49,097 --> 00:03:50,777
And a third one is a warning.

65
00:03:50,857 --> 00:03:54,737
For a whole hour before the clocks change,
it's switched to one.

66
00:03:54,997 --> 00:03:57,197
So your clock doesn't need to know
which Sunday in March

67
00:03:57,427 --> 00:04:01,357
the clocks go forward,
or which Sunday in October they go back.

68
00:04:01,877 --> 00:04:03,957
Nobody ever taught it the rule.

69
00:04:04,577 --> 00:04:06,917
It's simply told, an hour in advance.

70
00:04:07,747 --> 00:04:12,187
If Europe ever stops changing the clocks,
the warning will simply never come again.

71
00:04:14,707 --> 00:04:19,387
Another bit used to warn of a leap second,
an extra second added now and then,

72
00:04:19,467 --> 00:04:22,807
because the Earth's rotation
doesn't keep perfect time.

73
00:04:23,387 --> 00:04:25,967
That minute could have sixty-one seconds.

74
00:04:26,816 --> 00:04:29,516
The last one was added at the end of 2016.

75
00:04:30,096 --> 00:04:36,076
But in October 2026, the world's metrology
authorities decided to end leap seconds.

76
00:04:36,936 --> 00:04:41,416
From 20 May 2027,
UTC will run continuously.

77
00:04:44,136 --> 00:04:47,856
And the first fourteen bits
of every minute aren't about time at all.

78
00:04:48,213 --> 00:04:50,673
Since 2006, they've also been used

79
00:04:50,903 --> 00:04:55,073
to carry encrypted weather forecasts,
for compatible weather stations.

80
00:04:55,604 --> 00:04:58,284
So the signal that sets your alarm clock
could also tell

81
00:04:58,514 --> 00:05:01,844
someone else's weather station
whether it would rain on Thursday.

82
00:05:04,594 --> 00:05:07,654
And yet most of these clocks
don't listen all the time.

83
00:05:08,294 --> 00:05:13,294
Listening costs battery, so many of them
try once a night, in the early hours.

84
00:05:13,951 --> 00:05:17,111
Long waves reach a clock in two ways:
along the ground,

85
00:05:17,341 --> 00:05:19,531
and bounced off the upper atmosphere.

86
00:05:20,071 --> 00:05:24,811
After dark, that upper path changes,
and the signal often carries further.

87
00:05:25,183 --> 00:05:28,363
And at three in the morning,
fewer machines, chargers and lights

88
00:05:28,593 --> 00:05:31,143
are usually running
nearby to drown it out.

89
00:05:31,701 --> 00:05:35,181
Between those check-ins,
the clock runs on a quartz crystal,

90
00:05:35,341 --> 00:05:37,901
which can drift by a second or so a day.

91
00:05:38,474 --> 00:05:42,074
At the next successful check-in,
the radio corrects that drift.

92
00:05:44,634 --> 00:05:46,674
Even where it listens matters.

93
00:05:47,214 --> 00:05:50,314
The antenna is a small rod of ferrite,
wrapped in wire.

94
00:05:51,004 --> 00:05:52,824
It hears best when the rod lies across

95
00:05:53,054 --> 00:05:56,404
the direction of the transmitter,
not pointing at it.

96
00:05:56,744 --> 00:06:00,744
Turn the clock around,
and a weak signal can vanish completely.

97
00:06:01,191 --> 00:06:05,251
Which is why one clock works on a shelf,
and the same clock, two metres away,

98
00:06:05,481 --> 00:06:08,991
behind a reinforced concrete wall,
never sets itself.

99
00:06:11,791 --> 00:06:14,571
So where does the transmitter
get its time from?

100
00:06:15,173 --> 00:06:20,733
From PTB, Germany's national metrology
institute, which runs atomic clocks.

101
00:06:21,477 --> 00:06:24,517
The second itself is defined
by caesium atoms.

102
00:06:25,597 --> 00:06:34,717
One second is exactly
9,192,631,770 oscillations

103
00:06:34,947 --> 00:06:36,737
of the radiation they give off.

104
00:06:37,119 --> 00:06:40,519
So a cheap alarm clock on a bedside table
is, quite literally,

105
00:06:40,599 --> 00:06:43,959
following a time standard
defined by caesium atoms.

106
00:06:46,549 --> 00:06:48,149
Not perfectly, though.

107
00:06:48,809 --> 00:06:51,849
The signal travels at the speed of light,
and even that takes

108
00:06:52,079 --> 00:06:54,649
about three milliseconds
for every thousand kilometres.

109
00:06:55,969 --> 00:06:59,969
The receiver adds a delay of its own,
and your clock corrects for none of it.

110
00:07:00,369 --> 00:07:01,889
It doesn't need to.

111
00:07:02,189 --> 00:07:03,749
It only shows seconds.

112
00:07:06,139 --> 00:07:09,079
From one site near Frankfurt,
the signal reaches clocks

113
00:07:09,309 --> 00:07:11,879
up to about two thousand kilometres away.

114
00:07:12,739 --> 00:07:14,199
That's most of Europe.

115
00:07:14,877 --> 00:07:20,077
Switzerland had its own transmitter,
called HBG, until the end of 2011.

116
00:07:21,057 --> 00:07:24,757
Its antennas needed replacing,
too few people used it,

117
00:07:24,837 --> 00:07:27,857
and Swiss clocks could simply listen
to Germany instead.

118
00:07:28,586 --> 00:07:33,966
Others kept theirs. Britain, France,
Japan and China all run their own.

119
00:07:34,529 --> 00:07:37,809
And in North America, WWVB has broadcast

120
00:07:38,039 --> 00:07:43,369
from Fort Collins since 1963,
with a time code since 1965.

121
00:07:44,353 --> 00:07:46,333
Same principle, different dialect.

122
00:07:47,173 --> 00:07:49,713
A zero is a dip of two tenths of a second.

123
00:07:50,373 --> 00:07:52,113
A one lasts half a second.

124
00:07:52,473 --> 00:07:54,233
And instead of one gap per minute,

125
00:07:54,313 --> 00:07:58,273
a longer marker comes every ten seconds,
like commas in the sentence.

126
00:07:59,313 --> 00:08:01,033
Two in a row mean a new minute.

127
00:08:01,923 --> 00:08:04,323
The biggest difference
is which time it sends.

128
00:08:05,003 --> 00:08:10,083
Germany broadcasts Central European civil
time, shared by much of the continent.

129
00:08:10,639 --> 00:08:13,679
But the United States
spans several time zones.

130
00:08:14,719 --> 00:08:20,639
So WWVB sends universal time, UTC,
and you tell the clock where you are.

131
00:08:20,947 --> 00:08:22,767
It doesn't even send the month.

132
00:08:23,167 --> 00:08:27,380
Just the day of the year,
from one to three hundred and sixty-six.

133
00:08:27,680 --> 00:08:30,580
None of these stations
speak the same language.

134
00:08:31,160 --> 00:08:34,260
A clock built for Germany
can't make sense of Colorado.

135
00:08:36,670 --> 00:08:40,530
But on both sides of the Atlantic,
it's the same quiet routine.

136
00:08:41,330 --> 00:08:44,610
Night after night, a small receiver
lines itself up with an atomic

137
00:08:44,840 --> 00:08:48,710
time standard,
hundreds or thousands of kilometres away.

138
00:08:51,620 --> 00:08:53,520
So here's a question for you.

139
00:08:54,180 --> 00:08:58,040
If every radio time signal disappeared
tonight, would you even notice?

140
00:08:58,480 --> 00:09:00,110
Let me know in the comments.
